  "conventions": {
    "absent_vs_empty": "An empty cell in a required column is the empty string. An empty cell in an optional column is the absence of a value — never a stand-in for zero, for the empty string, or for a value that exists but was not recovered. An optional column never holds the empty string, which is what keeps the two readings apart.",
    "child_tables": "A field whose length varies per record is not a column: it is its own file, <domain>__<field>.csv, carrying its parent's key in its leading columns — the join_key each entry in files states. A variable-length field nested inside one of those recurses one level further out, <domain>__<field>__<nested>.csv, joined on the parent key plus the struct row's ordinal. A file with no parent and an empty join key is domain-wide rather than per-record: it is a lookup shared by every record, and its own key column names the entry.",
    "curves": "A curve is its own file with encoding, x and value columns. encoding is dense — one value per step, x counting from 1 — or keyframes, stating only the points the data gives; values between keyframes are not interpolated here. The axis is called x rather than difficulty because it is not always difficulty: some curves are indexed by level, item level or node rank, and the dataset leaves the axis unnamed rather than mislabelled.",
    "line_endings": "Rows end with LF, not RFC 4180's CRLF, because this repository's files are LF everywhere and a delivery that disagreed with its own repository would show a diff on every re-emit for files whose content did not change. A cell may carry a CR or an LF of its own inside quotes, so splitting a file on line breaks without honouring quotes reads one row as several; each file's row_count in files is the count a conforming reader gets.",
    "media": "A media handle expands to <field>_id, <field>_kind, <field>_width and <field>_height, and never a path. Map the id onto wherever you serve art from.",
    "non_finite_numbers": "CSV has no literal for these, any more than JSON does, so they appear as the strings NaN, Infinity and -Infinity — the same spellings the JSON delivery uses.",
    "order": "Records are stable-sorted and columns are in declaration order — the same order every other delivery states its fields in — so a data refresh diffs one entity at a time. Column order is part of what this schema_version promises.",
    "quoting": "RFC 4180: a cell containing a comma, a double quote, CR or LF is wrapped in double quotes and its own double quotes are doubled. Nothing else is quoted, so the unquoted form is what a diff of a data refresh reads."
  },
